---
import Work from "@layouts/Work.astro";

export const frontmatter = {
    title: "Chelsea Timber Library",
    date: "2022.04.27",
    coverImage: "/chelseatimberlibrary/Exterior Ground.jpg",
};
---

<Work
    title={frontmatter.title}
    subtitle="Crafted Timber, Urban Rhythm, and Public Life"
    description=""
    coverImage={frontmatter.coverImage}
    coverImageAlt={frontmatter.title}
    coverImageAlt={frontmatter.title}
    course="Integrated Design Schematic & Development"
    courseDescription="Reimagine The Branch Library"
    advisor="Florencia Vetcher & Ted Krueger"
    collaborators=""
    date={frontmatter.date}
    location="Chelsea, New York"
    gallery={import.meta.glob("/public/chelseatimberlibrary/gallery/*")}
>
    <section class="stack">
        <div class="row">
            <div class="stack">
                <p>
                    Located in the heart of Chelsea and adjacent to the High
                    Line, this public library is conceived as a social anchor—an
                    indoor counterpart to the city's most iconic elevated park
                    and a haven for intellectual growth. The project began as an
                    exploration of movement, once defined by a continuous
                    internal ramp. In the redesigned scheme, circulation becomes
                    more deliberate: the main building follows a clear
                    structural grid, while a secondary easement retains a hybrid
                    ramp-stair promenade. This exterior pathway offers long
                    landings for sitting and viewing the city, functioning as an
                    informal extension of the High Line experience.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The massing is shaped entirely by zoning constraints, with
                    frontage offsets determining height and form. Rather than
                    sculpting the volume further, the design prioritizes
                    structural clarity. A heavy timber construction
                    system—selected for its environmental performance,
                    longevity, and inherent warmth—creates thick load-bearing
                    walls that reduce reliance on columns and give the building
                    a grounded presence. While the surrounding context is
                    predominantly brick, the timber structure introduces a
                    contemporary yet compatible material expression. Generous
                    glazing brings daylight deep into the building, while the
                    southern façade remains intentionally opaque to protect the
                    privacy of neighboring residences.
                </p>
            </div>
            <img
                src="/chelseatimberlibrary/Exterior High Line.jpg"
                alt="Exterior High Line"
                style="width:50%"
            />
        </div>
        <img
            src="/chelseatimberlibrary/Preliminary Study.jpg"
            alt="Preliminary Study"
        />
        <p>
            Program distribution follows a gradient from energetic public to
            calmer private use. The basement houses cultural functions,
            including an auditorium, an open gallery, and classrooms for
            workshops. Library spaces begin on the first floor—elevated five
            feet above grade to address the site's moderate flood hazard—with
            children's materials and family-oriented areas. These spaces
            gradually transition to teens' collections on the second floor and
            adult materials on the upper levels. Through its zoning-driven
            massing, structural rigor, and layered circulation, the project
            positions the library as both refuge and urban connector—an
            architecture shaped by constraints, context, and the evolving needs
            of its community.
        </p>
        <img src="/chelseatimberlibrary/Long Section.jpg" alt="Long Section" />
        <img
            src="/chelseatimberlibrary/Short Section.jpg"
            alt="Short Section"
        />
    </section>
</Work>
